"Braess' Paradox (https://supernet.isenberg.umass.edu/braess/braess-new.html)\nciteturn7view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://supernet.isenberg.umass.edu/braess/braess-new.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 48\nL0: The Braess Paradox\nL1: cite0†What is the Braess Paradox? cite1†The Original Braess Article and its English Translation L2: \nL3: cite2†Removing Roads and Traffic Lights Speeds Urban Travel†www.sciam.com L4: Scientific American\nL5: February 2009 cite3†Traffic Panel†www.worldsciencefestival.com L6: World Science Festival\nL7: June 12, 2009 cite4†Playing in Traffic L8: American Scientist\nL9: July-August 2015\nL10: cite5†Braess on Broadway cite6†Gridlock†video.pbs.org L11: America Revealed PBS\nL12: February 2012 cite7†Braess Around the World L13: cite8†Braess Visit to the Virtual Center for Supernetworks at the Isenberg School of Management at UMass Amherst cite9†Braess Paradox in Other Network Systems cite10†Links L14: \nL15: Did you know that:\nL16: When 42nd street was closed in New York City, instead of the predicted traffic gridlock, traffic flow actually improved?\nL17: or\nL18: When a new road was constructed in Stuttgart, Germany, traffic flow worsened and only improved after the road was torn up? These paradoxical phenomena are but two real-life examples of the Braess paradox, named after Dietrich Braess who, in 1968, noted that, in a user-optimized transportation network, when a new link (road) is added, the change in equilibrium flows may result in a higher cost (travel time) to all travelers in the network, implying that users were better off without that link.\nL19: cite11†Image: Braess Paradox L20: The Braess Paradox Article and its English Translation\nL21: \nL22: In 1968, Professor Braess published his article in German, Über ein Paradoxon aus der Verkehrsplanung, in Unternehmensforschung (12, 258–268). A cite12†Copy of the German Article has been made available by Professor Braess. cite13†Image: Braess L23: cite14†Image: Braess In 2005, Professor Braess asked Professor Anna Nagurney of UMass Amherst to assist him in preparing an English translation of the article. Ms. Tina Wakolbinger, an Austrian native, then a Doctoral Student in Management Science at the Isenberg School of Management at UMass, assisted in the translation. The translated article was published in the November 2005 issue of the INFORMS journal Transportation Science. cite15†Click here for a copy of the translation.†homepage.rub.de L24: The Editor-in-Chief of Transportation Science at that time, Professor Hani S. Mahmassani, asked Professor Nagurney and Professor David Boyce of Northwestern University to write a cite16†Preface to the translation to put the work into a historical context. cite17†Image: Braess Intorduction L25: Braess Visits UMass Amherst\nL26: During April 5-8, 2006, Professor Braess visited the Isenberg School of Management at UMass Amherst. While in Amherst, he delivered his first ever cite18†lecture on the Braess Paradox in North America .\nL27: \nL28: Photos from the\nL29: visit of\nL30: Professor Braess, April 5-8, 2006\nL31: Click each photo\nL32:  to view a\nL33: larger version.\nL34: cite19†Image: Braess Around the World L35: Links\nL36: cite20†Homepage of Professor Dietrich Braess†homepage.ruhr-uni-bochum.de L37: cite21†What if They Closed 42d Street and Nobody Noticed?†www.nytimes.com Gina Kolata- The New York Times\nL38: December 25, 1990\nL39: cite22†Homepage of Professor Anna Nagurney†people.umass.edu cite23†Anna Nagurney at the World Science Festival†www.worldsciencefestival.com L40: cite24†Homepage of Professor Tina Wakolbinger†www.wu.ac.at cite25†Anna Nagurney on WGBY Connecting Point†vimeo.com L41:   cite26†Anna Nagurney at NY Times Energy for Tomorrow†futuretransport.us L42: cite27†Want less traffic? Build fewer roads!†plus.maths.org +plus Magazine\nL43: Braess Paradox in Other Network Systems\nL44: The Braess paradox is also relevant to other network systems in which the users operate under decentralized (selfish) decision-making behavior rather than centralized or system-optimizing behavior.\nL45: These include such network systems as: The Internet - cite28†Nagurney, Parkes, and Daniele (2007) Electric Circuits - cite29†Nagurney and Nagurney - EPL (2016) Electric power generation and distribution networks - cite30†Bjorndal and Jornsten†brage.bibsys.no - cite31†Witthaut and Timme Phys.org (2012)†phys.org Biology - Motter in New Scientist (2014) Basketball! - cite32†Skinner (2011)†arxiv.org L46: \nL47: Last update: December 26, 2016\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLightning Strikes Twice - NASA Science (https://science.nasa.gov/resource/lightning-strikes-twice/)\nciteturn7view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/lightning-strikes-twice/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 369\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com cite1†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2:   * Explore\nL3: \nL4: Search[Input][Input: Search...]\nL5:   * News & Events cite2†News & Events†www.nasa.gov L6:     * cite3†Recently Published†www.nasa.gov L7:     * cite4†Video Series on NASA+†www.nasa.gov L8:     * cite5†Podcasts & Audio†www.nasa.gov L9:     * cite6†Blogs†www.nasa.gov L10:     * cite7†Newsletters†www.nasa.gov L11:     * cite8†Social Media†www.nasa.gov L12:     * cite9†Media Resources†www.nasa.gov L13:   * Multimedia cite10†Multimedia†www.nasa.gov L14:     * cite11†NASA+†plus.nasa.gov L15:     * cite12†Images†www.nasa.gov L16:     * cite13†NASA Live†www.nasa.gov L17:     * cite14†NASA Apps†www.nasa.gov L18:     * cite15†Podcasts†www.nasa.gov L19:     * cite16†Image of the Day†www.nasa.gov L20:     * cite17†e-Books†www.nasa.gov L21:  