ars L279:     * cite70†Jupiter L280:     * cite71†Saturn L281:     * cite72†Uranus L282:     * cite73†Neptune L283:     * cite74†Pluto & Dwarf Planets L284:     * cite75†Asteroids, Comets & Meteors L285:     * cite76†The Kuiper Belt L286:     * cite77†The Oort Cloud L287:     * cite32†Solar System Home L288: # Lightning Strikes Twice\nL289: \nL290: NASA\nL291: \nL292: November 12, 2019\nL293: \nL294: Credit  | NASA\nL295: Language  |\nL296: \nL297:   * english\nL298: \nL299: View of a lightning bolt during the launch of Apollo 12 from Pad A, Launch Complex 39, Kennedy Space Center. The silhouetted structure is the mobile launch tower. This electrical discharge between clouds and the ground took place 36.5 seconds after lift-off when the Apollo 12 space vehicle was at about 6,000 feet altitude.\nL300: The Saturn V rocket was struck by lightning again at 52 seconds and caused an electrical disturbance. The mission was saved when flight controller John Aaron realized that normal readings could be restored by putting the Signal Conditioning Electronics (SCE) system on its auxiliary (AUX) setting, which meant that it would operate even with low-voltage conditions.\nL301: \nL302: Despite the two lightning strikes, the crew made it safely to the Moon and back home to Earth.\nL303: \nL304: Photograph taken on 14 November 1969.\nL305: cite191†Image: NASA Logo L306: ### National Aeronautics and Space Administration\nL307: \nL308: NASA explores the unknown in air and space, innovates for the benefit of humanity, and inspires the world through discovery.\nL309: \nL310:   * cite192†About NASA's Mission†www.nasa.gov L311: cite126†Join Us†www.nasa.gov L312:   * cite193†Home†www.nasa.gov L313:   * cite2†News & Events†www.nasa.gov L314:   * cite10†Multimedia†www.nasa.gov L315:   * cite11†NASA+†plus.nasa.gov L316:   * cite194†Missions†www.nasa.gov L317:   * cite195†Humans in Space†www.nasa.gov L318:   * cite31†Earth L319:   * cite32†The Solar System L320:   * cite33†The Universe L321:   * cite28†Science L322:   * cite196†Aeronautics†www.nasa.gov L323:   * cite197†Technology†www.nasa.gov L324:   * cite198†Learning Resources†www.nasa.gov L325:   * cite192†About NASA†www.nasa.gov L326:   * cite199†NASA en Español†www.nasa.gov L327: ### Follow NASA\nL328: \nL329:   * cite200†More NASA Social Accounts†www.nasa.gov L330:   * cite201†NASA Newsletters†www.nasa.gov L331: \nL332:   * cite202†Sitemap†www.nasa.gov L333:   * cite9†For Media†www.nasa.gov L334:   * cite203†Privacy Policy†www.nasa.gov L335:   * cite204†FOIA†www.nasa.gov L336:   * cite205†No FEAR Act†www.nasa.gov L337:   * cite206†Office of the IG†oig.nasa.gov L338:   * cite207†Budget & Annual Reports†www.nasa.gov L339:   * cite208†Agency Financial Reports†www.nasa.gov L340:   * cite127†Contact NASA†www.nasa.gov L341:   * cite209†Accessibility†www.nasa.gov L342: \nL343:   * Page Last Updated:\nL344: Feb 12, 2025\nL345: \nL346:   * Page Editor:\nL347: \nL348: Caela Barry\nL349: \nL350:   * Responsible NASA Official for Science:\nL351: \nL352: Dana Bolles\nL353: \nL354: Did you find what you were looking for?(Required)\nL355: \nL356: [Input] Yes\nL357: \nL358: [Input] No\nL359: \nL360: [Input] Partly\nL361: \nL362: What were you looking for?(Required)\nL363: \nL364: What were you hoping to accomplish by visiting our website?(Required)\nL365: \nL366: [Button: Submit] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL367: \nL368: [Button: × ]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIdentity, Persistence, and the Ship of Theseus (https://faculty.washington.edu/smcohen/320/theseus.html)\nciteturn7view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://faculty.washington.edu/smcohen/320/theseus.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 66\nL0: ## Identity, Persistence, and the Ship of Theseus\nL1: Heraclitus’s “river fragments” raise puzzles about identity and persistence: under what conditions does an object persist through time as one and the same object? If the world contains things which endure, and retain their identity in spite of undergoing alteration, then somehow those things must persist through changes. Heraclitus wonders whether one can step into the same river twice precisely because it continually undergoes changes. In particular, it changes compositionally.\nL2: At any given time, it is made up of different component parts from the ones it was previously made up of. So, according to one interpretation, Heraclitus concludes that we do not have (numerically) the same river persisting from one moment to the next.\nL3: Plato is probably the source of this “paradoxical” interpretation of Herclitus. According to Plato, Heraclitus maintains that nothing retains its identity for any time at all:\nL4: \nL5: > “Heraclitus, you know, says that everything moves on and that nothing is at rest; and, comparing existing things to the flow of a river, he says that you could not step into the same river twice” (Cratylus 402A).\nL6: \nL7: But what Heraclitus actually said was more likely to have been this:\nL8: > “On those who enter the same rivers, ever different waters flow.” (fr. 12)\nL9: On Plato’s interpretation, it’s not the same river, since the waters are different.  On a less paradoxical interpretation, it is the same river, in spite of the fact that the waters are different. On both interpretations of Heraclitus, he holds the Flux Doctrine: Everything is constantly altering; no object retains all of its component parts from one moment to the next.\nL10: The issue is: what does Flux entail about identity and persistence? Plato’s interpretation requires that Heraclitus held what might be called the Mereological Theory of Identity (MTI), i.e., the view that the identity of an object depends on the identity of its component parts. This view can be formulated more precisely as follows:\nL11: > For any compound objects, x and y, x = y only if every part of x is a part of y, and every part of y is a part of x.\nL12: \nL13: > I.e., an object continues to exist (from time t_{1} to time t_{2}) only if it is composed of all the same components at t_{2} as it was composed of at t_{1}. Sameness of parts is a necessary condition of identity.\nL14: It now seems that if we want to allow that an object can persist through time in spite of a change in some of its components, we must deny MTI. An object x, existing at time t_{1}, can be numerically identical to an object y, existing at time t_{2}, even though x and y are not composed of exactly the same parts.\nL15: \nL16: But once you deny MTI, where do you draw the line? Denying MTI leaves us vulnerable to puzzle cases, the mother of all of which is the following.\nL17: ##### The Ship of Theseus\nL18: \nL19: This is a puzzle that has been around since antiquity, probably later than Heraclitus, but not much later. It first surfaces in print in Plutarch (Vita Thesei, 22-23):\nL20: > “The ship wherein Theseus and the youth of Athens returned had thirty oars, and was preserved by the Athenians down even to the time of Demetrius Phalereus, for they took away the old planks as they decayed, putting in new and stronger timber in their place, insomuch that this ship became a standing example among the philosophers, for the logical question of things that grow; one side holding that the ship remained the same, and the other contending that it was not the same.”\nL21: Plutarch tells us that the ship was exhibited during the time [i.e., lifetime] of Demetrius Phalereus, which means ca. 350-280 BCE. (Demetrius was a well-known Athenian and a member of the Peripatetic school, i.e., a student of Aristotle. He wrote some 45 books, and was also a politician).\nL22: The original puzzle is this: over the years, the Athenians replaced each plank in the original ship of Theseus as it decayed, thereby keeping it in good repair. Eventually, there was not a single plank left of the original ship. So, did the Athenians still have one and the same ship that used to belong to Theseus?\nL23: But we can liven it up a bit by considering two different, somewhat modernized, versions. On both versions, the replacing of the planks takes place while the ship is at sea. We are to imagine that Theseus sails away, and then systematically replaces each plank on board with a new one. (He carries a complete supply of new parts on board as his cargo.) Now we can consider these two versions of the story:\nL24:   1. Simple version: Theseus completely rebuilds his ship, replaces all the parts, throws the old ones overboard. Does he arrive on the same ship as the one he left on? Of course it has changed. But is it it?\nL25: \nL26: > Let A = the ship Theseus started his voyage on.\nL27: > Let B = the ship Theseus finished his voyage on.\nL28: Our question then is: Does A = B? If not, why not? Suppose he had left one original part in. Is that enough to make A identical to B? If not, suppose he had left two, etc. Where do you draw the line?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMap Your Blind Splot (https://isle.hanover.edu/Ch03Eye/Ch03BlindSpot_evt.html)\nciteturn7view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://isle.hanover.edu/Ch03Eye/Ch03BlindSpot_evt.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 34\n"
